  "title": "Tops can hide any constraint problem",
  "subject": "Whether composing like cycles alone implies a polynomial-time constraint problem",
  "conclusion": "Every finite structure is homomorphically equivalent to a law that composes like cycles, and every finite core to a core that does, so composing like cycles alone admits NP-complete constraint problems, even among cores; the core of such a law need not compose like cycles, and it can be non-Taylor. With constants, or for a core with no nontrivial automorphism, composing like cycles does give polynomial time, and both hypotheses are needed.",
